# Datasets and model artefacts — never committed, often gigabytes
data/raw/
data/images/
# The prepared dataset is gigabytes and the manifest is a local-path index
# of a machine that no longer exists -- but `splits.csv.gz` is five columns
# saying which photograph went to which split, which is the only artefact of
# a training run that CANNOT be regenerated (GBIF moves, so a rebuild gives
# a different dataset). Losing it with the first pod made check_splits.py's
# clean result unverifiable after the fact. Same `**` + negation shape as
# ml/runs below, for the same reason: git will not descend into a directory
# ignored with a trailing slash, so the negations have to sit under `**`.
data/processed/**
!data/processed/
!data/processed/splits.csv.gz
# Training outputs. The checkpoints are ~330 MB each and GitHub refuses
# anything over 100 MB, so the directory is ignored by default -- but the
# run-book's step 9 says to COMMIT the small text artefacts, because they
# are the evidence for the release decision. Those are un-ignored below.
#
# Without the negations this rule silently swallowed MODEL_CARD.md and
# report.json on the first real run, at the point where the pod was still
# billing and the files existed nowhere else.
ml/runs/**
!ml/runs/
!ml/runs/*/
!ml/runs/*/MODEL_CARD.md
!ml/runs/*/report.json
!ml/runs/*/calibration.json
!ml/runs/*/labels.json
!ml/runs/*/config.yaml
!ml/runs/*/history.json
# ~4 MB of float16 predictions on the held-out split. Bigger than its
# neighbours and the reason any later question about a run is answerable at
# all: the first run's deadly probability masses were computed, used and
# discarded, and the pod that held them was destroyed.
!ml/runs/*/predictions.npz
# And the same on the validation split. A safety threshold tuned on the
# split that gates release is fitted to its own gate; this is the one
# it may honestly be tuned on.
!ml/runs/*/predictions-val.npz
*.parquet
*.pt
*.pth
*.onnx
*.tflite

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Node / Expo
node_modules/
.expo/
dist/
*.log
# ...except a run's training log. `nohup ... > train.log` is the only record
# of the early epochs: run four's 0 and 1 scrolled off the one terminal that
# ever held them, and the shape of those first epochs is exactly what rule
# 6's checkpoint selection turns on. 2.5 KB.
#
# This negation has to sit AFTER the `*.log` above rather than with the
# ml/runs negations, because the LAST matching pattern wins -- placed
# earlier it is overridden and `git add` exits 0 having added nothing.
# That is the fourth time a gitignore rule has silently swallowed an
# artefact of a paid run; this one was caught by `git status` on the pod.
!ml/runs/*/train.log

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Incident reports are real user data, never committed
data/incidents*.jsonl

# scripts/try_it.sh keeps its service logs here
.try_it_api.log
.try_it_web.log

# Real photographs: the manifest and results are kept, the images are not.
# They are large, personal, and phone photographs carry location metadata.
eval/photos/**
!eval/photos/
!eval/photos/.gitkeep
